From 13c503286f6e2a320f3708852ec2a82e36091111 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: rkvenc forward-port <forward-port@local>
Date: Tue, 21 Jul 2026 08:50:22 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] video: rockchip: rga3: serve over-4G memory on RGA2 via
 DMA-API mapping
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The RGA2 MMU consumes raw 32-bit page addresses, so the driver
rejected every job whose memory sits above 4G — a common situation on
large-memory boards without dma32 heaps, where system-heap and malloc
buffers land above 4G by allocation luck. The vendor page-table fill
already anticipated the fix (its dormant use_dma_address branch
describes swiotlb producing device-compatible addresses) but nothing
ever exercised it.

Adopt the upstream V4L2 rga model — let the DMA API place or bounce
memory below 4G against the 32-bit RGA2 device:

- add page-granular DMA mapping variants that accept multi-segment
  mappings (the MMU walks per-page entries, so the single-IOVA-span
  contract of the RGA3 path does not apply; each segment is still
  validated against the 32-bit register format);
- map per-job (non-handle) buffers for RGA_MMU schedulers with those
  variants and fill page tables from sg_dma_address(), which is
  identical to the physical address below 4G and a swiotlb bounce
  above it;
- for handle buffers imported for another core, create a transient
  per-job mapping against the RGA2 device (dma-buf re-attachment or a
  fresh pinned-pages table for virtual imports), fill the page table
  from its DMA addresses, and release it when the job's buffers are
  put; after the copy-back, clean the origin pages through their
  persistent mapping so a later cache invalidate cannot discard the
  bounced data;
- only reject over-4G memory outright when it is physically
  contiguous (addressed directly, bypassing the MMU) or a raw
  physical import; the scheduler policy now keeps RGA2 as a candidate
  for remappable buffers, and a mapping failure at commit time (e.g.
  swiotlb pool exhaustion) fails the job with EOPNOTSUPP and a log
  pointing at below-4G buffers.

Bounces cost a CPU copy per direction, so below-4G (CMA/dma32)
buffers remain the documented fast path; this is a correctness and
compatibility net, not a performance feature.

Signed-off-by: rkvenc forward-port <forward-port@local>
